The Rust binding links the installed static `libpancake` directly and presents a
typed API in
[`bindings/rust/src/lib.rs`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/rust/src/lib.rs).
You work with a `Peer` and Rust enums for `Reference`, `Argument`, and `Event`, so
the wire's value tree arrives as ordinary Rust values you can match on.

## Async

The async surface is [tokio](https://tokio.rs/), behind an opt-in `async` feature. An `AsyncConnection`
runs the sans-io peer over a `tokio::net::TcpStream`. Because the peer is a
single-owner state machine, the `Connection` is `!Send`: you create and use it on
one task inside a `LocalSet`, which keeps the peer calls synchronous and lets only the
socket reads and writes await. A `tell` returns once the deliver is queued on that
driver task, and the task flushes it.

## Actors

The actor layer is an `actors::Vat` over `FnMut(&str, &[Argument])` closures. A
local actor is a Rust closure, not a foreign callback, and a leading symbol
argument is the method verb. An eventual-send to a partner-hosted reference travels
the wire and settles through `Vat::send(...).await`.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build rust-dogfood    # cargo test against libpancake, sync + async
```

The full surface lives in the
[Rust binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/rust/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/rust/client.rs)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
